      "id": "04b6b591-d67b-4bca-8a53-494a354b4dc9",
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "Excellent — **Section 14.5** is the point where *everything clicks*.\nThis file completes Java’s **core functional interfaces** by adding **Function-like behavior and Supplier**.\n\nI’ll explain this in **clear blocks**, focusing on **what new ideas are introduced**, not repeating earlier ones.\n\n---\n\n# 🧠 Big picture of Section 14.5\n\nThis section teaches you:\n\n1. **Function-style transformations** (`replaceAll`, `setAll`)\n2. **Index-based lambdas**\n3. **Supplier<T>** → generate values *on demand*\n4. How Java APIs internally use functional interfaces\n\nYou now know **all the major functional interfaces**.\n\n---\n\n## 1️⃣ `replaceAll` → UnaryOperator / Function\n\n```java\nlist.replaceAll(s -> s.charAt(0) + \" - \" + s.toUpperCase());\n```\n\n### What is `replaceAll` using?\n\nInternally:\n\n```java\nUnaryOperator<T>\nT apply(T t);\n```\n\nYour lambda:\n\n```java\ns -> s.charAt(0) + \" - \" + s.toUpperCase()\n```\n\n### What happens to the list?\n\nBefore:\n\n```\nalpha\ncharlie\ndelta\n```\n\nAfter:\n\n```\na - ALPHA\nc - CHARLIE\nd - DELTA\n```\n\n🔑 **Rule**\nIf a method:\n\n* takes one value\n* returns the same type\n  → it uses `UnaryOperator<T>` (a special `Function<T,T>`)\n\n---\n\n## 2️⃣ Arrays basics (warm-up)\n\n```java\nString[] emptyStrings = new String[10];\nSystem.out.println(Arrays.toString(emptyStrings));\n```\n\nOutput:\n\n```\n[null, null, null, null, null, null, null, null, null, null]\n```\n\nThen:\n\n```java\nArrays.fill(emptyStrings,\"\");\n```\n\nOutput:\n\n```\n[\"\", \"\", \"\", \"\", \"\", \"\", \"\", \"\", \"\", \"\"]\n```\n\nThis prepares the array for **functional filling**.\n\n---\n\n## 3️⃣ `Arrays.setAll` → IntFunction\n\n### First example\n\n```java\nArrays.setAll(emptyStrings, (i) -> \"\" + (i + 1) + \". \");\n```\n\n### What Java passes to the lambda\n\n* `i` = array index (0 → length-1)\n\nSo results:\n\n```\n1. , 2. , 3. , ...\n```\n\n### Functional interface used\n\n```java\nIntFunction<T>\nT apply(int value);\n```\n\n🔑 **Index-based lambdas** are common in Java APIs.\n\n---\n\n## 4️⃣ More complex `setAll` using `switch`\n\n```java\nArrays.setAll(emptyStrings, (i) -> \"\" + (i + 1) + \". \"\n    + switch (i) {\n        case 0 -> \"one\";\n        case 1 -> \"two\";\n        case 2 -> \"three\";\n        default -> \"\";\n    }\n);\n```\n\nThis shows:\n\n* Lambdas can contain **expressions**\n* Lambdas can use **modern switch**\n* Logic stays **localized**\n\nResult:\n\n```\n1. one\n2. two\n3. three\n4.\n5.\n...\n```\n\n---\n\n## 5️⃣ Supplier<T> — the star of this section ⭐\n\n```java\nSupplier<Integer>\n```\n\n### What is Supplier?\n\n```java\nT get();\n```\n\n* Takes **no arguments**\n* Returns a value\n* Used for **lazy / random / dynamic generation**\n\n---\n\n## 6️⃣ Method using Supplier\n\n```java\npublic static String[] randomlySelectedValues(\n        int count,\n        String[] values,\n        Supplier<Integer> s) {\n```\n\n### How this works\n\n```java\nselectedValues[i] = values[s.get()];\n```\n\nEach call to:\n\n```java\ns.get()\n```\n\nproduces **a new index**.\n\n---\n\n## 7️⃣ Passing a Supplier lambda\n\n```java\nString[] randomList = randomlySelectedValues(\n        15,\n        names,\n        () -> new Random().nextInt(0, names.length)\n);\n```\n\n### Lambda explanation\n\n```java\n() -> new Random().nextInt(0, names.length)\n```\n\nMeans:\n\n* no input\n* generate a random number every time\n\nEquivalent to:\n\n```java\nInteger get() {\n    return randomIndex;\n}\n```\n\n🔑 **Supplier = value factory**\n\n---\n\n## 8️⃣ Why Supplier is powerful\n\nWithout Supplier:\n\n```java\nint index = random.nextInt();\nuse index\n```\n\nWith Supplier:\n\n```java\n\"Give me a new value whenever I ask\"\n```\n\nThis is used heavily in:\n\n* streams\n* caching\n* lazy initialization\n* dependency injection\n\n---\n\n## 9️⃣ Full functional interface map (now complete)\n\n| Interface           | Method        | Use                   |\n| ------------------- | ------------- | --------------------- |\n| `Consumer<T>`       | `accept(T)`   | do something          |\n| `BiConsumer<T,U>`   | `accept(T,U)` | do something with two |\n| `Predicate<T>`      | `test(T)`     | filter                |\n| `Function<T,R>`     | `apply(T)`    | transform             |\n| `UnaryOperator<T>`  | `apply(T)`    | transform same type   |\n| `BinaryOperator<T>` | `apply(T,T)`  | combine               |\n| `Supplier<T>`       | `get()`       | generate              |\n\n---\n\n## 🧠 Final mental model (VERY IMPORTANT)\n\n> **Java APIs don’t contain logic — they ask YOU for logic**\n\nThey do this using:\n\n* lambdas\n* functional interfaces\n* generics\n\n---\n\n## 🚀 What you’re now ready for\n\nYou now understand:\n\n* Streams (`map`, `filter`, `reduce`)\n* Method references\n* Spring callbacks\n* Event-driven Java\n* Reactive programming basics\n\nIf you want, next I can:\n\n* rewrite this using **method references**\n* show **real Stream API examples**\n* explain **how Spring uses Supplier & Function**\n\nJust say the word 👍\n",
      "metadata": {
